<http://data.silknow.org/object/b70eb0f5-b9f6-3f76-82f4-ae7fa9a577b9/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "This 1890s dinner dress with its distinctive design of clusters of oranges on a black ground would have made a dramatic effect when worn. The realistic design was created using sensitive colour combinations and a highly sophisticated jacquard-weaving technique, and was probably executed by silk manufacturers in the Spitalfields area of London.\n\nThe fabric shows the influence of the Far Eastern, and particularly Japanese styles. A taste for Japanese art and design was stimulated by the International Exhibition held in London in 1862, during which many people saw the country's wares for the first time. The contrasting colours and clearly defined motifs in the fabric design are typical of Japanese style, while the dress construction conforms to the fashionable Western silhouette of the late nineteenth century."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/b70eb0f5-b9f6-3f76-82f4-ae7fa9a577b9> .

<http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390>
        a                         ecrm:E39_Actor , ecrm:E40_Legal_Body ;
        rdfs:label                "Victoria and Albert Museum" ;
        ecrm:P1_is_identified_by  "Victoria and Albert Museum" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://isni.org/isni/000000045904354X> , <https://viaf.org/viaf/128776887> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/b70eb0f5-b9f6-3f76-82f4-ae7fa9a577b9>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Jacquard-woven silk dress with black ground and pattern of oranges, and black machine-made lace and silk ribbon panels in front"@en , "Dinner dress made of jacquard-woven silk with a realistic design of clusters of oranges on a black ground, trimmed in front with silk ribbon and black machine lace. The bodice is supported with whalebone strips."@en , "This 1890s dinner dress with its distinctive design of clusters of oranges on a black ground would have made a dramatic effect when worn. The realistic design was created using sensitive colour combinations and a highly sophisticated jacquard-weaving technique, and was probably executed by silk manufacturers in the Spitalfields area of London.\n\nThe fabric shows the influence of the Far Eastern, and particularly Japanese styles. A taste for Japanese art and design was stimulated by the International Exhibition held in London in 1862, during which many people saw the country's wares for the first time. The contrasting colours and clearly defined motifs in the fabric design are typical of Japanese style, while the dress construction conforms to the fashionable Western silhouette of the late nineteenth century."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/1b7e3e9d-b424-3451-a41b-8c608089d04c> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/053953d7-796e-3be6-b3ed-9b6c3e916afa> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/8cc46e52-5da8-3bed-8984-ab4f9d2a1a6d> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/7127f51b-b4f9-3fb0-87ca-b3795b198a1e> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/702f38ae-6191-35f0-b3e0-2fb14529b693> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/58db5df5-5d3d-3199-8bca-26d9c8ea99ff> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/3ed1fd2d-a9bf-377d-b565-83877dba66ff> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This 1890s dinner dress with its distinctive design of clusters of oranges on a black ground would have made a dramatic effect when worn. The realistic design was created using sensitive colour combinations and a highly sophisticated jacquard-weaving technique, and was probably executed by silk manufacturers in the Spitalfields area of London.\n\nThe fabric shows the influence of the Far Eastern, and particularly Japanese styles. A taste for Japanese art and design was stimulated by the International Exhibition held in London in 1862, during which many people saw the country's wares for the first time. The contrasting colours and clearly defined motifs in the fabric design are typical of Japanese style, while the dress construction conforms to the fashionable Western silhouette of the late nineteenth century."@en , "Dinner dress made of jacquard-woven silk with a realistic design of clusters of oranges on a black ground, trimmed in front with silk ribbon and black machine lace. The bodice is supported with whalebone strips."@en , "Jacquard-woven silk dress with black ground and pattern of oranges, and black machine-made lace and silk ribbon panels in front"@en ;
